{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789759271,"nonce":"qzpgv0HziYjZ2DSFBXbkXbovjT0=","message":"Root cause found on the NUC. The crash was never the AOT binary's: it was the child `hl` the suite spawns (`TestSys.run` uses `Sys.command`, so the child's stderr reaches the parent's output, and the harness classified the banner as the parent's crash while the utest tally read 1118/1118).\n\nThe child runs `ExitCode.hl`, which calls `Sys.exit(code)`. `hlp_sys_exit` went through `std::process::exit`, i.e. libc `exit()` with atexit handlers -- the path the CLI's own return deliberately avoids (`exit_without_atexit`). LLVM's atexit handler destroys the `PassRegistry` on the main thread while the beadie broker thread is still inside `LLVM_InitializeAllTargets` -\u003e `registerPass` -\u003e `DenseMap::grow`. Under load the broker's start is delayed enough for the two to overlap; glibc then reports `corrupted size vs. prev_size while consolidating`, `double free or corruption (out)` or `free(): invalid pointer` and aborts with 134, which the test records as \"expected 255 but it is 134\".\n\nIt was hidden further by native-call recovery: the SIGSEGV that sometimes precedes the abort was siglongjmp'd back into the interpreter, which then touched destroyed TLS and aborted from `hlp_remove_trap_jit` -- the trace in the first core. `ASH_NATIVE_RECOVERY=0` (added) shows the real site.\n\nReproducer: 14 busy loops on the 16-core NUC, then `ash bin/hl/ExitCode.hl 255` in a loop -- 3 of 400 exit 134. With `Sys.exit` routed through the same flush-and-`_exit` path as the CLI: 0 of 1000, and the sys suite passes 3/3 under aot and hybrid under the same load.\n\nThe harness now reports a banner with an ordinary exit code as \"a child process crashed\" (FAIL) rather than CRASH, since ash's handler re-raises and a real crash always dies by signal.","files":null}]}